WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Scott Shawyer will return to competition in January, 2027, teaming up with Alan Roberts for the RRRC Transatlantic Race on the foiling IMOCA Emira. This is Offshore North Episode 11. One person is trying to become the first Canadian to sail alone, non-stop around the world. On the Canadian boat, the team's YB tracker shows Emira 4 alongside in Gossport, UK, Port Smith Harbor as of October 26, 2026. Canada Ocean Racing announced this week that Scott Shawyer will team up with British Offshore Sailor Alan Roberts

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.700
Roberts for the RORC transatlantic race in January 2027.

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.600
The pair set a double-handed course record together in 2023.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:42.680
They will sail the 3,000 nautical mile crossing from Lanzarote to Antigua on the foiling

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:43.360
Emirafor.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.680
The post notes that Shire is looking ahead after the decision not to compete in the

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:48.140
route to rum.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.940
The announcement includes a photo of Alan shaking hands with Scott from their 2023 record

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:53.200
run.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:57.800
Shire is described as already planning his next opportunity to race after missing the

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:58.560
route to rum start.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.660
The team states that the crossing will allow him to get back on the water and build valuable

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:04.260
experience aboard Emirafor.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.620
This gives Shire another double-handed offshore passage on the boat that will carry his

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.400
Vendée Globe 2028 campaign.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.340
Every mile sailed on Emirafor builds the solo and systems experience required for grade

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:16.600
two qualification races.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:22.020
The RORC transatlantic counts as a qualifying passage under the published IMOSA rules

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:23.860
for the 2028 Vendée Globe.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.400
Completing it on Emirafor would satisfy the requirement to finish at least one grade

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.900
two solo or double-handed race on the boat that will be used in the Vendée Globe within

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:37.380
twice the winner's time. The campaign's public goal remains the first Canadian finish in

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:44.200
the 2028 edition, which starts on November 12, 2028. The RRC transatlantic pairs weather-rooting

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:48.280
decisions across the North Atlantic with the same pressure systems I read from the flight deck.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:55.666
Now, the fleet. The expert panel assembled by sailors for the 2026 route to rum IMOC class named

00:01:55.666 --> 00:02:00.626
Jeremy Bayou, the clear favorite, followed by Sam Goodchild and Ambrogio Bacaria.

00:02:01.106 --> 00:02:03.666
Four of the six skippers pulled Pig Bayou on Chiral.

00:02:04.246 --> 00:02:09.606
The panel included the last two root-derum IMO say winners, Paul Mailhot from 2018 and

00:02:09.606 --> 00:02:16.526
Thomas Ruyant from 2022, plus Yuan Risham, Nicholas Lunven, Romain Atanasio and Justine

00:02:16.526 --> 00:02:16.846
Metro.

00:02:17.386 --> 00:02:21.986
The panel noted a drop to 22 IMOK entries from the record 38 in 2022.

00:02:21.986 --> 00:02:27.306
to Thomas Ruyant attributed that drop to companies hesitating on long-term sponsorship commitments.

00:02:27.846 --> 00:02:32.046
Ruyant said the projects are built around long-term commitments, so as soon as confidence

00:02:32.046 --> 00:02:34.766
in the future starts to waver, the impact is felt immediately.

00:02:35.286 --> 00:02:39.706
He added that companies are finding it difficult to commit for the long-term, yet paradoxically

00:02:39.706 --> 00:02:42.306
enthusiasm for what the sailors do has not diminished.

00:02:42.806 --> 00:02:46.466
The panel agreed that around 10 skippers have a realistic chance of a podium finish

00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:47.706
or a top five result.

00:02:48.306 --> 00:02:52.986
Nicklaus Lunven said he was blown away by Charles' performance in the Transat Cafe lower,

00:02:53.426 --> 00:02:57.866
where the boat posted significantly higher average speeds than rivals downwind in the trade winds.

00:02:58.446 --> 00:03:01.926
Lunven highlighted the team's work on the rudder system that helps lift the stern,

00:03:02.386 --> 00:03:05.566
calling it a clever solution that delivers a significant performance gain.

00:03:06.246 --> 00:03:10.266
Thomas Ruyant added that Jeremy has come through a season in which he has continued to learn

00:03:10.266 --> 00:03:15.486
a lot about Charles, whose hull underwent a major refit last winter. The reduced field still

00:03:15.486 --> 00:03:17.926
contains roughly 10 realistic podium contenders.

00:03:18.366 --> 00:03:22.726
The economic pressure described by Ruyant affects every campaign's ability to keep boats

00:03:22.726 --> 00:03:25.426
competitive through the 2028 Vendée Globe cycle.

00:03:26.026 --> 00:03:30.566
A smaller entry list does not reduce the technical demands on the boats, or the skippers who must

00:03:30.566 --> 00:03:32.306
manage them alone across the Atlantic.

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:37.930
Skippers were presented to the public in St. Malo on 26 October, ahead of the 1 November

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:38.190
start.

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:42.390
Violet Dorange received the strongest reception among the IMO sailors.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:47.130
Charles Cudrilier reclaimed the trophy after the mascot ran off with it during the

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:51.530
Altum presentation. The two vintage classes kick things off, followed by the

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:55.930
Class 40s. On stage, each sailor was accompanied by a child clutching an

00:03:55.930 --> 00:04:00.250
origami paper boat carrying a dream. The sailors from St. Mallow in the Ocean

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:04.890
50 class seemed to have a clear advantage in the applause, and energized Alexia

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:09.110
Barrier burst onto the stage to represent the Altum class, escorted by the eight

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:09.770
other sailors.

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:12.950
Codgerly Air said anything is possible on the route to rum.

00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:16.370
The event marks the final public gathering before the fleet sales.

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:21.130
The visible enthusiasm shows how the route to rum functions as the sports largest single

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:24.910
race festival, even when entry numbers are lower than in previous editions.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:29.110
The presentation sequence gives every class its moment in front of the crowd before the

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:30.510
boats head out across the Atlantic.

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:32.130
Time for plain sailing.

00:04:33.205 --> 00:04:37.325
Double-handed offshore racing requires two people to share every task that a solo skipper

00:04:37.325 --> 00:04:38.185
must perform alone.

00:04:38.185 --> 00:04:43.545
In the RORC transatlantic, the pair must keep the boat moving at all times, manage sail

00:04:43.545 --> 00:04:47.925
changes, repairs and routing decisions, and still find time for sleep in short watches.

00:04:48.505 --> 00:04:49.825
The boat itself does not change.

00:04:50.325 --> 00:04:55.365
The same foiling IMOXA that will race solo in the Vendée Globe must be handled by two instead

00:04:55.365 --> 00:04:55.725
of one.

00:04:56.285 --> 00:05:00.685
The key difference is that one person can always be on deck while the other rests, which reduces

00:05:00.685 --> 00:05:03.285
the extreme sleep fragmentation of solo skipper accepts.

00:05:03.945 --> 00:05:08.045
This format therefore serves as useful preparation for the longer solo races that count

00:05:08.045 --> 00:05:09.665
toward Vendée Globe qualification.

00:05:10.065 --> 00:05:15.425
The 3,000 nautical mile distance from Lanzarote to Antiga forces the pair to manage the

00:05:15.425 --> 00:05:20.105
same weather systems and boat systems that appear in solo transatlantic races, yet the workload

00:05:20.105 --> 00:05:20.705
is divided.

00:05:21.265 --> 00:05:25.365
A solo skipper on the same boat would face 20-minute sleep cycles and full responsibility

00:05:25.365 --> 00:05:27.185
for every decision at 3 in the morning.

00:05:27.785 --> 00:05:32.025
Double-handed racing lets the team test those systems and that boat in real ocean conditions

00:05:32.025 --> 00:05:33.705
without the full solo burden.

00:05:33.705 --> 00:05:40.516
and the countdown. 748 days remain until the Vendée Globe start on November 12, 2028.

00:05:41.176 --> 00:05:46.456
The campaign's next concrete milestone is the RORC transatlantic race in January 2027.

00:05:47.136 --> 00:05:51.316
That's your week in offshore racing. Full sources in the episode notes. I'll see you

00:05:51.316 --> 00:05:56.296
next Monday. I'm Dan, fair winds and eyes on the horizon. Quick tip from the network.

